Rapid growing coronavirus-affiliated coronavirus venom (COVID-19) turbulence in the European Union and in the U.K. between January 1 and March 15, 2020
Kumulative incidence of coronavirus infection (COVID-19) has similar trends in the countries of the European/Europe economic zone and in the UK, thereby confirming that, despite the different stages of its development in different countries, the COVID-19 pandemic is growing rapidly in all countries.
Taking into account the experience of Italy, countries, hospitals and intensive care units, it is necessary to increase the level of prepared-up influencies of COVID-19 patients who will need treatment and, in particular, intensive therapy.
On December 31, 2019, several cases of pneumonia unknown etiology were recorded in Wuhan of the Chinese province of Hubei.
On January 9, 2020, the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention reported that the causative agent of infection is a new type of coronavirus, now known as a coronavirus of severe acute respiratory syndrome type 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
From this moment, the disease caused by SARS-CoV-2 began to be called a coronavirus infection (COVID-19).
According to data available to date, in about 80 percent of people with COVID-19, the disease proceeds in a mild form, i.e. in the form of a respiratory tract infection with or without pneumonia, while the majority of patients recover.
Approximately in 14% of cases, COVID-19 develops in a disease of a more severe form with the need to be hospitalized, and in the remaining 6% takes a critical form when intensive therapy is required.
Mortgage rates of patients hospitalized with COVID-19 is approximately 4%.
This study analyzes the cumulative morbid incidence of COVID-19 in each country of the European Union (EU) / European Economic Area (EEA) and in the United Kingdom and compares them with trends in the Chinese province of Hubei.
We are also comparing the current number of COVID-19 cases in the EU/EEA and in the U.K. with Italian data for the period from January 31 to March 15, 2020.
COVID-19 cases in the EU/Ei-Ei-eMes
After the outbreak in China, COVID-19 has swapped to other countries, and now in the rest of the world the COVID-19 pandemic develops according to the same dynamics as in China.
On March 11, 2020, the Director-General of the World Health Organization (WHO) declared the COVID-19 pandemic.
On March 5, 2020’s March 5, 2020 Eurosurveillance and his colleagues reported the first confirmed cases of COVID-D-19 in Europe, which met the criteria by which the WHO determines the presence of the disease.
The first three confirmed cases in the EU/EEA were registered in France on January 24, 2020 - all three patients returned from Wuhan of the Chinese province of Hubei.
As of March 15, 2020 cases of COVID-19 were detected in all 30 EU/EEA and in the U.K. countries, while 39,768 cases of infection and 1,727 deaths have been recorded between December 31, 2019 and 1,727 of which 1,750 cases, including 1,441 with a fatal outcome, fell on Italy alone.
Determination of cumulative quantity and cumulative vestiment of COVID-19
The European Center for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC) daily at 8/00 renovates data on the incidence of COVID-19 cases in every country of the world, obtained from official sources, such ministries of health of these countries, national and regional health authorities and WHO.
These data are used to analyze trends in the dissemination of COVID-19 in the EU/EEA and in the United Kingdom to compare them with data on Italy.
As an indicator of the prevalence of active cases of COVID-19, the truncated turbulent incidence of COVID-19 for 14 days was calculated, taking into account the normal course of COVID-19 in each EU/EEA and in the United Kingdom for the period from 1 January to 15 March 2020.
We have also presented the cumulative number of cases recorded in each country at 8:00 on March 15, 2020, compared with data on Italy for the period from January 31 to March 15, 2020.
COVID-19 Development Trends in EU/EEA Countries and the United Kingdom
Tendencies in truncated cumulative COVID-19 cumulative incidence over a 14-day period in the EU/EEA and in the U.K. generally corresponded to trends observed in the Chinese province of Hubei (Fig. 1).
Cumulative incidence of COVID-19 in the EU/EEA and in the U.K
This was due mainly to a rapid increase in the number of reported cases in Italy, but in all other EU/EEA countries and in the United Kingdom there have been similar tendencies to an increase in cumulative morbid vestiness of COVID-19 (additional material).
In Figure 2, the cumulative number of COVID-19 cases in the EU/EEA and in the United Kingdom, compared with the same figure for Italy for the period from 31 January to March 15, 2020.
According to these data, the total number of cases already registered as of 8:00 on March 15 in 15 other EU/EEA and in the United Kingdom, is comparable to the amount recorded in Italy only about 3 weeks earlier.
Our results indicate a rapid increase in the number of COVID-19 reported cases in the EU/EEA and in the U.K
Observed trends of cumulative velocity of COVID-19 suggest that the pandemic is developing at a comparable rate in all countries.
And this is despite different phases in different countries, the dissimilarity response measures taken by national public health authorities, and, perhaps, different criteria for recognizing cases of the disease and different patient selection rules for COVID-19, including “contributing” testing.
In the beginning of March 2020, doctors in the affected regions of Italy reported that approximately 10 per cent patients with COVID-19 require intensive care, and the media noted that hospitals and intensive care units in these regions are already as filled.
At the moment, data on the influencies of patients with COVID-19 in hospitals and (or) intensive care unit in the EU/EiZ are only available for 6% and 1% of cases, respectively (data are not provided).
However, it is necessary to carry out a systematic collection of such information in order to supplement the current observation data, the subject of which is the number of registered cases and the number of deaths.
According to the results of a study conducted in 2010-2011 in Europe there is a significant scatter in the number of bellies in intensive care units and intensive care units for patients: from 29.2 in Germany to 4.2 beds per 100,000 people in Portugal.
This means that other countries may have more or less resources than Italy (12.5 beds in intensive care units and intensive care units per 100,000 population in 2010-2011).
ECDC's sixth updated report on the results of rapid risk assessment for COVID-19 presents scenarios for modeling the loading of health-reliant health system resources with estimated data on hospitalization of COVID-19 infections in each EU/EEA and in the United Kingdom, when the risk of a shortage of available beds in intensive care units exceeds 90 percent.
Since the cases are currently grouped in certain regions of the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom, and hospitals and intensive care departments usually serve the population of a certain territorial group, information on cases of infection and the number of beds in intensive care units is recommended, if possible, as far as possible, provided for units of level 2 of territorial units for statistical purposes (NUTS-2).
The experience of Italy and the current trends in other countries show that the COVID-19 pandemic is growing rapidly in the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom.
Therefore, countries, hospitals and intensive care units should be prepared for the SARS-CoV-2 contact infection scenario, unbalancing for a long period of time, and for an increase in the number of patients with COVID-19 who are in need of medical care and, in particular intensive care, which, for example, can be observed in the affected regions of Italy.
As reported in the latest ECDC report on the results of rapid risk-assembrbezzlement, the operational, proactive and integrated approach is of great importance to curb the spread of SARS-COV-2, followed by a shift from restraining the consequences, since, taking into account a projected rapid increase in the number of diseases in decision-making individuals and hospitals may simply not have enough time to comprehend, take and appropriately adjust their responsive measures if such measures are not taken in advance.
The report also lists public health measures to mitigate the impact of a pandemic.
Countries only have a short period of time during which they can intensify their control efforts to slow the circulation of SARS-CoV-2 and reduce the burden on their health care systems.
If this is not to be done, in the coming days or weeks the health systems in other EU/EEA countries will face an influx of patients who will need intensive care.
An outbreak of coronavirus infection 2019 (COVID-19), Caused by a coronavirus of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) type 2 (SARS-CoV-2), has become a tragedy for humanity: at the same time, more than 3 000 people have died in China and other countries of the world, and the number of those infected has exceeded 80,000.
Looks similar to the homogeneous SARS-CoV virus, which caused severe acute respiratory syndrome in thousands of people in 2003, SARS-CoV-2 may also transmit from bactericles and provocative symptoms, using a similar mechanism.
However, COVID-19 is characterized by a lower severity of the disease and a lower mortality rate than SARS, but it is much more contagious and affects older people more often than young people, and men are more likely than women.
In response to a rapid increase in publications about a new disease, this article proposes an up-to-date and comprehensive overview of the rapidly developing subject of the study.
We will consider the basic aspects of epidemiology, etiology, virology, diagnosis, treatment, prognosis of a further course and the prophylare of this disease.
“Although the answers to many of the questions have yet to be found, we hope that this review will help to understand and exacerbate a dangerous disease.
The Feast of Spring (Chinese New Yie year), which in 2020 fell on January 25, became an unprecedented and unforgettable event for all Chinese who, in connection with the outbreak of a new viral infection urging to stay at home for the whole holiday and for many weeks after.
Taking into account the high degree of similarity with the coronavirus (CoV), which caused an outcrease of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) in 2003, the World Health Organization (WHO) on February 11, 2020, called this SARS-CoV-2 virus, and a concomitant disease - a coronavirus infection 19 (COVID-19).
Epidemic began in Wuhan, the Chinese city of Wuhan and quickly spread throughout the country, followed by almost 50 other countries in the world.
According to March 2, 2020 was detected more than 80 000 confirmed cases of COVID-19, more than 40 000 patients recovered, more than 3 000 patients died.
WHO Warns COVID-19 is “number-one eigenies for society” and is potentially more dangerous than terrestrial.
According to PubMed (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubed/), in less than two months there were more than 200 COVID-19 publications, including articles about its virology, epidemiology, etiology, diagnostics and treatment, beginning with the first report dated January 7, 2020, which determined the sequence of a virus isolated from a number of patients.
This review is an attempt to comprehend the results of the research in a new and rapidly developing subject area.
To the extent possible, we will try to compare COVID-19 with SARS and another coronavirus-caused disease of Middle-Wedregtic Respiratory Syndrome (MERS), whose outbreak occurred in 2012.
We will also discuss currently known facts about the prevention and anticipation of the course of the disease, as well as some other equally pressing issues.
The coronavirus has traditionally belonged to pathologies that are not deadly to a person and mainly cause approximately 7% of total respiratory diseases.
Nevertheless, in this centenary we have twice encountered highly pathogenic human coronaviruses, i.e. coronavirus severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) and the coronavirus of the Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS-CoV), which caused an outbreak of diseases initially in China in 2003 and in Saudi Arabia in 2012, respectively, respectively, and soon spread.
Therefore, the current COVID-19 is the third outbreak of coronavirus infection in the history of mankind.
As shown in Fig. 1.1, a message about clusters of pneumonia of unknown origin was first received by the National Health Commission of the People's Republic of China from Wuhan on December 31, December 31, 2019.
After seven days, a genetic sequencing of the virus was released.
On January 15, 2020, the first fatality was registered in Wuhan.
In the meantime, the epidemiology quickly spread to neighboring cities, provinces and countries.
On January 20 it became known about the infection of medical workers, which made it possible to predict the possibility of transmitting the virus from a person to a person.
Quarantine was quarantined in Wuhan on January 23, city public transport ceased operations.
January 24, according to the first clinological study of this disease, it was noted that in 21 of the 41 coronavirus-affiliated patients had direct contacts with the seafood market in Wuhan, which was considered the starting point of spread of infection from an unknown animal.
On January 30, the WHO announced that the coronavirus outbreak was a global public health emergency.
At the time of the preparation of this report, the disease spread throughout China and has already infiltrated in about 50 other countries around the world (Figurate 2).
Taking into account the rampant development of the situation, the final scale and degree of risk of an outbreak have yet to be determined.
On February 11, 2020, according to the results of a multi-center clinical study, in which 8 8,866 patients took part, including 4 021 patients with confirmed COVID-19, the following updated epiphan development pattern (https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/UlBi-HX_rHAPXa1qHA2bhdA).
Influencing SARS-CoV-2 were exposed to all age groups, but mainly affected people between the ages of the ages of 30 and 65.
Almost half (47.7%) of those infected were over the age of 50, very few are under the age of 20, and only 14 infected are under the age of 10.
SARS-CoV-2 among men is higher (0.31/100,000) than among women (0.27/100 000).
COVID-19 was propagated by clusters, mainly in Hubei province and in nearby regions.
On the average from the ongoing symptoms of COVID-19 before the diagnosaltion took 5 (2-9) days.
The incubation period averaged 4.8 (3.0-7.2) days.
From the moment of manifestation of symptoms before the onset of the onset of death on an average of 9.5 (4.8-13) days.
The basic indicator of reproduction (R0) was 3.77 (95% CI: 3.51-4.05), and the adjusted R0 was 2.23–4.2.
By January 23, 2020 there was an exponentiary increase in the number of infected, which coincided with large-scale transport transportation on the eve of the Chinese New Year.
Mortality among patients with a cognonated diagnosis accounted for 1,44% (95% CI – 1.10-1.86%), and an adjusted mortality rate among patients – 3.06% (95% CI: 2.02-4.59%).
The three harsaturating risk factors for COVID-19 infection were: sex (male), age (≥ 60) and severe pneumonia.
The coronaviruses are a subfamilia of large envelentireal viruses, containing a single strand of segregation RNA.
They can be diverged into four species, i.e. alpha, beta, gamma and delta, from which alpha and betacoronaviruses are known to affect people.
In the case of coronavirus lesions of the SARS and MERS glycoprotein of the enlargement spike yield (S), it binds to cellular receptors of angiotensin-contact enzyme type (ACE2) and dipeptidyl peptidopedase-4 (DPP4), respectively, after which the memabrannic synthesis occurs.
The cytoplasm is released by the cytoplasm genome; after replication of the viral genomic RNA, together with the envelope glycoproteins and nucleocapside proteins, generates virion-containing vesicle, which then merge with the cell membrane, releasing the virus.
The first genomic Sequence reports SARS-CoV-2 appeared on January 10, 2020.
SARS-CoV-2 has been found to be a new type of beta coronavirus whose genome is 99.98% coincided with 10 sequential specimens collected in the first outbreak in Huangan seafood in Wuhan.
Genetically SARS-CoV-2 closer to SARS-CoV than MERS-CoV
With sciatic electron microscopy, SARS-CoV-2 particles have been detected in ultra-thin slits of human respiratory epithelium.
It has been established that the human enzyme ACE2 is a receptor for SARS-CoV-2, as well as for SARS-CoV.
However, SARS-CoV-2 is more quilted with a human ACE2 enzyme weaker than in SARS-CoV, which explains the fact that SARS-CoV-2 causes a disease of a lesser severity in patients than SARS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 can also generate a previously unidentified short-living protein enclosed or f3b and a secreted protein encoded orf8.
Protein orf3b SARS-CoV-2 can play a role in terms of pathogenicity of the virus and suppress IFNβ expression; however, orf8 does not contain a known functional domain or motif.
On February 18, 2020, a team of authors led by Zhou presented reports on cryo-electric tomography of the structure of a full-size ACE2 enzyme with a resolution of 2,9 Å in combination with an amino acid conveyor B0AT1.
They found that this complex, which included open and enclosed conformations collected as a dimer, was assembled, and that a ACE2-B0AT1 complex could bind two S-proteins, which serves as evidence when releasing a coronavirus and infected.
B0AT1 can become a therapeutic target when conducting drug screening to suppositor the SARS-CoV-2 infection.
Destinies and Intermediate Host
It is known that both SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV were bacterial mice, from which the virus was transmitted to a person through a covetic and camellipots, respectively.
By phylogenetic comparison of SARS-CoV-2 with other coronaviruses it has been found that the first owners of SARS-CoV-2 are bats, since the new virus by 96% identical to two coronavirus-similars similar to SARS, namely voltavirus coronaviruses SL-CoVZX45 and SL-CoVZX21 bats.
However, it remains unidentified which species has become an intermediate owner, thanks to which the virus managed to overcome a species barrier and infect a person; the transmission pathway also has yet to be clarified.
Mr. Ji (Ji) with colleagues suggested that the carriers of the virus from bacteric jars to humans became snakes, during which there was a homogeneous recombination in the S-protein.
According to the results of their study, Chinese scientists from Guangzhou suggested that pangolines - long-digged mammals that feed on thugs and which are often used in traditional Chinese medicine - are the potential intermediate hosts of SARS-CoV-2; this assumption is based on a 99% genetic similarity between pangolin coronavirus and SARS-CoV-2.
However, the difference of the 1% distributed between the two genomes is still very large, so it is believed that the final results with concrete evidence have not yet been obtained.
The physical and chemical properties of SARS-CoV-2 are mostly still unknown.
In the laboratory, SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV can smash for 48 hours in a dried medium and up to 5 days at a temperature below 20 °C and 40-50% humidity.
SARS-CoV-2 may have similar properties.
It is known that SARS-CoV-2 is Sensitive to ultraviolet and heating up to 56 °C for 30 minutes; ether can effectively block the activity of the virus, 75 percent ethyl alcohol, chlorine-containing disinfectants, peruxary acid, chloroform and other fatty solvent but not chlorhexidine.
The human population as arguably does not have immunity to SARS-CoV-2, so people are susceptible to a new virus.
At the present time, there is no information about detailed studies of the immune response to SARS-CoV-2.
Thus, we can refer only to earlier studies of other coronaviruses, primarily SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV (fig.
As a rule, the virus that enters the body of the owner first recognizes an innate immune system by means of image-recognitive receptors (ORPs), including C-type lectin receptor, Toll-similar receptacles, NOD-similar receptors and RIG-like receptors.
According to the various mechanisms, the virus provokes the expression of inflammation factors, the maturation of dendritic cells, and the synthesis of type I interferons, which restrain the spread of the virus and accelerate phagocytosis of macrophages against viral antigens.
However, thanks to N-protein SARS-CoV can avoid an immune response.
An adaptive immune reaction is activated in the fight against the virus.
T-lymphocytes, including CD4 + and CD8+ T cells, play an important role in protecting the body.
CD4+ T cells stimulate the generation of virus-special antibodies in B-cells, and CD8+ Cell Cell Cells Directly Kill Cell Cell Cells.
T-helpers produce anti-inflammatory cytokines that help the protective cells.
However, the coronavirus can inhibit the functions of T-cells, causing their programmed death.
Humoral immunity, which includes components such as C3a and C5a and antibodies, also plays an important role in the fight against a viral infection.
For example, antibodies isolated from a re-recoverable patient were neutralized by MERS-CoV.
On the other hand, the hyperreaction of the immune system entails the local formation of a large number of free radicals, which can lead to serious damage to the lungs and other organs, and in the worst-case scenario will lead to organ insufficiency and even death.
SARS-CoV-2, which is characterized by a cluster manifestation, is more likely to affect older adults with concomitant pathologies and pregnant women.
Probability of infection is higher for those people who are exposed to a large number of viruses or have imperturbation of the immune system.
According to the results of the study of the data on the first 425 cases of infection in Wuhan, the estimated average incubation period SARS-CoV-2 is from 1 to 14 days, mainly 3-7 days.
However, the study of 1,099 cases showed that the incubation period lasted an on average for 3 days, while the scattage range ranged from 0 to 24 days.
A later study described above, based on demography 8,866 cases showed that the incubation was 4.8 (3.0-7.2) days.
For health authorities, it is very important to adjust the timing of quarantine measures introduced, taking into account the most accurate calculations of the duration of the incubation period, which will to prevent the spread of the virus by those infected in which the disease is asymptomatic.
A general practice has become a 14-day quarantine for persons who have had contacts with a virus or infected with the virus.
Should quarantine be extended for up to 24 days?
The main and the first symptom of COVID-19 is often a high temperature, which can be the only manifestation or is accompanied by other symptoms, such as dry cough, difficulty breathing, muscle pain, dizziness, head pain, sore throat, runny nose, diarrhea, ider, tar, vomit and vomit.
In some patients a week after the onset of the disease there was presidile and (or) hypoxia.
In severe cases, the disease progressed rapidly, and patients developed acute respiratory disease, septic shock, metabolic acidosis and coagolopathy.
Patients with a high fever and (or) symptoms of a respiratory disease and acute fever should be checked for the virus for early diagnosis, even in the absence of pathologies in the pictures of the thorax.
A demographic study, carried out at the end of December 2019, revealed the following indicators of the prevalence of symptoms: 98% - a high fever, 76% - dry cough, 55% - shortness of meals and 3% - diarrhoea; 8% of patients required artificial pulmonary ventilation.
Similar results were obtained as a result of the last two studies of cases of infection in the family and the transmission of the virus from asymptomatic infected.
Comparable results were obtained in a 2012 study of patients with MERS-CoV, whose main symptoms also had high fever (98%), dry cough (47%) and shortness of breath (55%).
However, 80% of them required artificial lung ventilation, this figure is much higher than among patients with COVID-19, and it corresponds to a higher share of MERS mortality than COVID-19.
In patients with MERS, diarrhoea (26%) and sore throat (21%).
The main symptoms of patients with SARS are a high temperature (99-100%), a dry cough (29-75%), shortness of breath (40-42%), diarrhoea (20-25%) and a sore throat (13-25%), and artificial pulmonary voluntencies require 14-20% of patients.
As of February 14, COVID-19 deaths accounted for 2%, and the total number of confirmed cases in the world reached 66,576.
Compared with this indicator, SARS mortality by November 2002 amounted to 10 per cent of 8,096 confirmed cases.
In the case of MERS, a demographic study conducted in June 2012 showed a mortality rate of 37% of 2 494 confirmed cases.
According to an earlier study of a study (R0) for SARS-CoV-2, was determined at 6.47 with a confidence interval (CI) within 5.71–7.23, while R0 for SARS-CoV was in the range from only 2 to 4.
Comparison SARS-CoV-2 with MERS-CoV and SARA-CoV by symptoms, mortality rates and R0 is shown in Table 1.1.
The foregoing indicators indicate a higher SARS-CoV-2 ability compared to MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV, but the mortality rate in case of infection with the new virus is lower compared to the last two.
Thus, it will be much more difficult to contain the SARS-CoV-2 epidemic than MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV epidemic.
Cluster manifestation often takes place when infected within a single family or a group of people who have gathered together for some reason or found themselves together in any transport, for example on a cruise liner.
Patients often traveled to Wuhan or to other affected regions, lived there or have been in contact with those infected or with those infected for the past two weeks prior to the onset of the disease.
However, according to the data presented in the reports, people can be carriers of the virus without any symptoms for more than two weeks, and the cured patients, after being discharged from the hospital, can once again become carriers of the virus, and this is an alarming signal to extend the quarantine period.
At an early stage, in patients a normal or decreased number of leukocytes (especially lymphocytes) in the peripheral blood.
For example, 1,099 patients with COVID-19 had lymphopenia with leukocytes < 4×109/L, including those of leukocytes < 1 × 109/L, as well as increased levels of astragine-aminotransferase and virhemassia.
An increased levels of liver enzymes, muscular enzymes and myoglobin have been found in the blood of some patients, and most of the patients experienced elevated C-reactive protein and erythrocyte sedimentation in their blood cells.
In patients with a severe course of the condition of the course of the condition of the disease, the level of D-dimer, the decay of fibrin present in the blood was increased, and the number of lymphocytes continually decreased.
In most patients with COVID-19 during x-ray examination of the thorses, pathologies were found, characterized by unequivourable dimmements on both sides or dimming according to uterine dimmary glass in the lung.
Patients often develop atypical pneumonia, acute pulmonary lesions and acute respiratory disease.
With the development of acute respiratory infections uncontrollable inflammation, accumulation of fluid and progressive fibrosis lead to a significant impaired gas exchange.
Dysfunction of pneumocytes of type I and II reduces the level of surf-acting compounds and increases surface tension, thus reducing the ability of the lungs to enlarge and increase the risk of pulmonary decrection.
Thus, the most notoriety of x-ray results often coincide with the most severe cases of the disease.
On February 18, 2020 the first pathological analysis of COVID-19 demonstrated pneumatic pneumatic detachment, the formation of hyaline membranes, and intertrench lymphocytic infiltration, and multi-nucular syncytial cells in patients who have died from the disease are consistent with pathologies of a viral infection and acute respiratory infections, and are analogous found in patients with SARS and MERS.
AS the main criterion for dihancing COVID-19 was the method of detecting SARS-CoV-2 by means of a polymerase chain reaction with reverse trancritase (PCR reverse tracranizes).
However, taking into account the high level of false-negative results, which can accelerate the development of the epidemic since February 13, 2020 in China began to make a diagnosis on the basis of clinical manifestations (more not relying exclusively on PCR with reverse trancentake).
A similar situation was the diagnosis of SARS.
Thus, in order to effectively diaghial, it is critical and it is necessary to reconcile these medical histories, clinical manifestations, laboratory tests, and X-ray results.
On February 14, 2020, a team of specialists led by Mr. Feng Zhang (Feng Zhang) described the SHERLOCK procedure protocol, based on repetition of CRISPR to detect SARS-CoV-2; this technique allows you to Identify Synthetic Scre Features of SARS-CoV-2 at a density of 20 × 10–18 mol / l to 200 × 10–18 mol / l (10–100 copies).
This new technique, if it is successfully tested on clinical drugs, can significantly increase the sensitivity of the tests and the ease of their conduct.
Due to the lack of experience in countering a previously unknown coronavirus, doctors can mainly provide patients with COVID-19 only supporting therapy, at the same time trying to apply any therapeutic methods that have been used or proposed in the treatment of other coronaviruses such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, as well as other viral diseases (Table 2).
These therapeutic methods involve the current or potential use of antiviral drugs, immunity nuufflers, steroids, plasma of recovered patients, traditional Chinese medicine, and the provision of psychological support.
For the treatment it was proposed to use even the plasma of the recovered patients.
Pharmaceutical companies are hurried to develop antibodies and vaccinates to fight the virus.
At the initial stages SARS-CoV-2 attacks mainly the lungs, as well as, perhaps, to a lesser extent it affects other organs that express ACE2, such as the gastrointestinal uterus and kinies.
Nevertheless, malnutrition and respiratory failure are the main threat and the cause of the death of patients.
Thus, in order to alleviate the symptoms and to save the lives, it is vital to support respiratory function, including general oxygen therapy, the supply of a high concentration of oxygen, non-invasive artificial ventilation and invasive pulmonary ventilation, which are prescribed according to the severity of the course of the disease.
Patients with severe respiratory symptoms need support in the form of uteral membranic oboxigenation (ECMO), a modified method of extrapulmonary circultation, which is used for the treatment of cardiac or respiratory insufficiency that poses a threat to the life of heart or respiratory insufficiency.
In addition, maintaining water-salty balancing, the prevention and treatment of secondary infections and syptic hazard, as well as the protection of the functions of life-important is essential for SARS-CoV-2.
It is known that the cytokine storm is a consequence of the hyper-regulation of the immune system of patients infected with SARS and MERS viruses.
The cytokine Storming is a form of a systemic inflammatory process that develops in responding to a series of cytokines, including TNFα, IL-1β, IL-2 IL-6, IFNα, IFNβ, IFNγ and MCP-1.
These cytokines provoke immunocytes for the release of a huge number of free radicals, which become the main reason for the development of acute respiratory infections and polyorganic insufficiency.
In the treatment of cytokine storm, in particular in patients in a serious condition, the suppression of immunity is crucial.
For the treatment of cytokine storm used corticosteroids and toccilimusab, monoclonal antibodies that serve as an interleukin-6 inhibitor.
Among the other methods of treating cytokine aching storm, based on the suppression of the immune system, the modulation of an immune reaction aimed at T-cell control; the blockade of cytokine cytokine cytokines, IL-1, and TNF; the suppression of yanus kinase; the use of blynutumoma, cytokine suppress suppositors of cytokine signs 4 and histon deacetalase inhibitors.
To reduce the degree of severity of the consequences of inflammatory processes in the treatment of SARS viruses, Steroid Scholars are widely used as immune suppressors.
However, steroids in large dosage doses do not bring benefits in the treatment of severe pulmonary urinations in patients with SARS and COVID-19.
Contrast, they can cause serious side effects, especially avascular osteonecrosis, which significantly aggravate the prognosis.
Nevertheless, short corticosteroid courses in small and medium dosages are recommended for use with caution in treating patients with COVID-19 in a critical condition.
At the time of compiling of this document there is no confirmed effective antiviral therapy.
However, the intravenous administration of remdesivir, the nucleotide analogue, turned out to be an effective treatment of an American patient with COVID-19.
Remdesivir is a recently developed antiviral drug originally developed by Gilead to treat Ebola virus-cauterated and Margburg virus.
Subsequently, remdesivir also demonstrated the possibility of sifting other viruses from single-strequent RNA viruses, including MERS and SARS viruses.
Relying on this data, Gilead provided this medicinal line-up to China to carry out paired studies on patients infected with SARS-CoV-2, and the results of these studies are eagerly awaited.
In addition, barycithinib, α interferon, lopynvir/ritonavir and ribavirin were suggested as possible treatment options for patients with acute respiratory symptoms.
Combined treatments using lopinavir / ritonavirus can be accompanied by diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, lesions of the liver and other side effects.
It is necessary to carefully monitor the interaction of these preparations with other drugs prescribed to patients.
Plasma Resurrected Patient and Antiplatelet Determination
Blood sampling in patients cured of contagious disease for the treatment of other patients who suffer from the same disease has been used for a very long time to protect a healthy population for a long time.
And in fact, in the blood of recovered patients often contains a relatively high number of antibodies to combat the pathogen.
Antibodies are in immunoglobulin, produced by B-lymphocytes to fight pathogens and other foreign objects; they recognize individual pathobloddics molecules and purposefully neutralize them.
On the basis of this prematurity, plasma was isolated from blood samples of a group of patients recovered from COVID-19, which was later administered to 10 seriously ill patients.
Within 24 o'clock, their symptoms have improved, the acuity of inflammation and a viral load have decreased, and blood saturation with oxygen has also increased.
However, in order to propose to massively apply this method prior to specific therapies, not yet carried out the check and not compiled expedited explanations are required.
In addition, taking into account the the therapeutic effect, certain deficiencies associated with the use of plasma should be carefully studied.
For example, antibodies can excessively stimulate the immune system and cause cytokine eruption syndrome, which, taking into account the toxicity, poses a potentially life-saturation threat.
Concentration of antibosses in the blood is usually low, and there is a lot to treat patients in a critical condition.
Difficulous to develop and produce specific antibodies quickly enough to overcome the global epidemiology.
Thus, it is more and more practical to isolate B cells of recovered patients and to determine the genetic code of effective antibodies or to perform scrining in order to find effective antibodies against critical proteins of the virus.
So we will be able to immediately switch to the mass production of antibodies.
For millennities, the Chinese used traditional medicine to treat a variety of diseases for millennia.
However, its effectiveness largely depends on the combination of many components of the formula, the composition of which differs depending on the diagnosis of the disease, based on the theories of Chinese traditional medicine.
Most effective components are still unidentified or have an indeterminated effect, as it seems difficult to isolate and verify these components or their optimum combination seems difficult.
At the present time, due to the lack of an effective specific therapy of COVID-19, Chinese traditional medicine has become one of the main alternative methods of treating patients with a mild and moderate severity of symptoms or for patients recovering from a severe stage of the disease.
For example, Suh Feng Jie Du capsules and Lian Hua Qing Wen capsules were found to be effective in the treatment of COVID-19.
The largest share of recovered patients with COVID-19 was observed in a number of provinces of China, in which Chinese traditional medicine was used to treat 87% of patients, including Gansu (63.7%), Ningxi (50%) and Hunan (50 per cent), while in Khubei province, where Chinese traditional medicine was used to treat only about 30 per cent with COVID-19.
However, this is a fairly rough comparison, since the assessment should be carried out taking into account a variety of other factors of influence, for example, the number of patients and the severity of their illness.
On February 18, 2020, Mr. Boli Zhang (Boli Zhang) and his colleagues published the materials of a study, as part of which a comparison was carried out exclusively according to the methods of Western medicine and combined treatment according to the methods of Western medicine and Chinese traditional medicine was carried out.
They found that the period of normalization of body tempos, the elimination of symptoms and the provision of medical care in the hospital was significantly less in patients, for the treatment of which a combination of methods of Western and Chinese traditional medicine was used, than in patients who were treated only for Western methods.
The most surprising is the fact that the share of patients who began to deteriorate symptoms (from a mild to a severe condition) was noticeably lower in a group that was healed by a combination of methods of Western and Chinese traditional medicine than in a group that was treated only for Western methods (7.4% compared to 46.2%), and the mortality rate in the first group was lower than in the second (8.8% compared with 39%).
Nevertheless, the efficacy and safety of Chinese traditional medicine still requires more carefully controlled studies conducted on a larger scale and in more regions.
It is also a certain interest to obtain, if possible, a description of a mechanism of action and an explanation of aspects of the efficacy of the components used in the methods of Chinese traditional medicine, or their combination.
Patients who are suspected of or confirmed having COVID-19 mainly have a strong fear of acutely contagious and even a deadly disease, and people closed for quarantine also feel bored, loneliness and extreme irritation.
In addition, symptoms such as fever, hypoxia and cough, as well as side effects of treatment, such as insomnia from corticosteroids, can cause even greater anxiety and psychological stress.
In the early stages of an influencing the disease caused by the SARS Virus, manifestations of a number of psychiatric diseases, including Chronic depression, increased anxietmaturity, punitive brethren, psychotic arousal symptoms, delirium, and even suicidal behavior.
Mandatory communication and quarantine tracking, which are among the measures taken by health authorities to curb the COVID-19 epidemic, can exacerbate people’s anxietmation and guilt associated with the spread of infection, quarantine and social stigma on their families and friends.
In this regard, psychological and psychiaic assistance should be provided to patients with COVID-19, persons under suspicion and in contact with them, as well as to all the rest of the people who are required.
As part as part of psychological support, multidisciplinary psychiatric care brigades should be established, to establish strict information with regular and accurate updating of information about the SARS-CoV-2 epidemic and treatment plans, and the use of professional electronic devices and applications in order to avoid close face-to-face contact.
Efficient Vaccinations play an important role to interrupt the chain of transmission from animal-carriers of infection and infected people to susceptible owners and are often used in addition to antiviral therapies used to restrain epidemiorgies caused by new viruses.
Work was carried out to create S-protein-based Vaccinations aimed to produce long-lived and highly active neutralizing antibodies and (or) protective immunity to SARS-CoV.
In the course of animal studies have developed lively weakened vaccines against SARS viruses.
However, before the start of clinical studies it is still necessary to determine the efficacy of these possible vaccines in natural conditions when they are used in age-related patients, a model of infection with lethal doses and the degree of protection against infection with zonotic viruses.
Perhaps the reason is the fact that the SARS virus disappeared 17 years ago and has not yet been reported about any new case since then.
At the same time, sporadic cases and clusters of disease caused by the MERS virus originating in the Middle East and are spreading to other regions due to conserving zoonotic sources in endemic habitats continue to emerge.
Vaccination strategies have been developed to combat MERS using an inactivated virus, plasmid of DNA, viral vectors, nanoparticles, virus-similar and recombinant protein elements, and some of these strategies were evaluated in animals.
The development of a safe and effective SARS-CoV-2 vaccine for persons who do not have immunity is an urgent and critical task that requires a solution to restrain a raging epidemic.
However, there are serious difficulties due to a long period (an on average 18 months) necessary for the development of a vaccine, and a dynamic modification of the coronavirus.
Being a previously unknown disease, COVID-19 is only starting to show a complete picture of the clinical course of the disease at the expense of thousands of patients.
In most cases, patients can gradually recover without complication.
COVID-19, however, like SARS and MERS, is also associated with a high frequency of complications and a high mortality rate among patients with a severe course of the disease.
In this connection, it is critical for health authorities to develop a predictive model of the disease in order to prioritize their measures, especially in areas where there is a shortage of resources.
Based on these clinical studies presented at the moment, the following factors may have an influencing the prognosis of the course of the disease in patients with COVID-19 or be associated with it (Table 33):
Age: Age was the most important factor in prognoying the disease caused by the SARS virus, which also corresponds to the truth for COVID-19.
COVID-19 mainly affects patients between the ages of 30 and 65, 47.7% of whom are over the age of 50, according to the above-mentioned study of 8,866 cases.
In patients who needed intensive therapies were more likely the presence of initial diseases and complications, and they were much older than those patients who didn’t need such therapy (announced 66 years of age versus age), suggesting that age was a prognosis factor for patients with COVID-19.
Floor: SARS-CoV-2 infection among men is higher than among women (0.31/100 000 against 0.27/100 000), as mentioned above.
morbidity and complications: in patients with COVID-19 who require intensive care, it is more likely to develop acute myocardial and arrhythmia.
Kardiological phenomena have also been the leading Cause of SARS patients dying.
SARS-CoV-2 has also been reported to be associated with ACE2-positive calangiocytes, which can cause liver malnutrition in patients with COVID-19.
It is worth noting that the age and initial diseases are closely interrelated and can distort the results.
Deviations revealed in laboratory studies: the level of C-reactive protein in the blood reflects the severity of the inflammatory process or tissue damage, and it is proposed to be considered as a potential prognostic factor in the course of the course of the disease, the reaction to therapy and the final recovery.
It was also proposed to take into account the relationship between C-responsive protein, the severity of the disease and the prognosis in COVID-19.
In addition, an increased level of lactat dehydogenesis, asparatine aminotransferase, analine aminotransferase and creatine kinases can also predict the result.
These enzymes in large quantities are excreted by various organs, especially the heart and liver, and their release occurs with tissue damage.
Therefore, they are traditionally markers of cardiac or hepatic dysfunction.
The main clinical Symptoms: To prognate the results and complications in the treatment of COVID-19 along with other factors should take into account x-ray examination of the thoracic and the development of clinical imminent symptoms over the course of time.
Using Steroids: As already described above, steroids are immunosuppressive suppressors, widely used in infectious diseases as an additional therapy in order to reduce the severity of the inflammatory process.
Since corticosteroids were widely used to treat patients with severe SARS forms, many of the survivors developed avavascular osteonecrosis, which caused a lifelong disability and a low quality of life.
Thus, if it is necessary to use steroids for the treatment of patients with COVID-19, these drugs should be prescribed in small dosages and short courses.
Psychological stress: As already described above, on the background of the COVID-19 epidemic, many patients suffer from unforeseen severe stress, since they often have to withstand long quarantine periods, cope with a high degree of uncertainty and observe the deaths of their relatives and other patients.
To relieve the stress of such patients and to help them return to a normal life, psychological consultations must necessarily be consulted and long-term support will be provided.
According to currently conducted demographic studies, the COVID-19 epidemic seems to be different from the SARS epidemic.
In addition to reclication in the lower respiratory tract, SARS-CoV-2, such as other coronaviruses that cause ordinary colds, can successfully multiply in the upper respiratory tract and cause mild symptoms or do not cause symptoms at an early stage of infection.
For this reason, those patients whose early stage of the disease has not yet graduating from the incubation period can, leading their usual life, spread the virus on a significant scale, which significantly complicates epidemiological control.
However, it was believed that SARS-CoV virus transmission occurs when patients are seriously ill, while in most cases it did not happen at an early stage of infection.
Thus, today's COVID-19 epidemiology is much more serious than the SARS epidemic and it is more difficult to control.
At the present time, a lot of work is being carried out, within the framework of which a general quarantine has been announced in Wuhan and neighboring cities, as well as the quarantine regime has been extended for almost the entire population, introduced in order to intertiprate the spread chain of SARS-CoV-2.
Although these measures cause great harm to the economy and other spheres of life of the country, the number of identified new patients is declining, which indicates a slowdown in the spread of the epidemic.
According to the most optimistic estimates, the outbreak of the disease will end by March, and the attenuation phase will last for 3-4 months.
However, some of the experts are not so optimistic.
Mr. Paul Guner (Paul Hunter) and his colleagues estimated that the COVID-19 outbreak, which looks much more contagious than SARS, will not end in 2020.
Researchers from the group led by Ira Longini (Ira Longini), compiled a model to predict the end of the epidemic and suggested that the SARS-CoV-2 virus can affect two-thirds of the world's population.
A group of Canadian specialists reported that the SARS-CoV-2 virus was detected in smears of the nasal and thong, taken from patients who have recovered and were discharged from hospitals two weeks earlier, which indicates that the new virus can become a cyclical disease similar to influenza.
However, a decrease in the number of new cases in China is encouraging, pointing out that currently applied strategies may have had an effect.
According to initial prognoses, Eball fever was supposed to affiliate up to a million people and caused the death of half a million patients.
But due to severe quarantine and isolating, the disease eventually managed to take under control.
There is a possibility that, like the SARS-CoV virus, SARS-CoV-2 influencing ability can weaken, and over time it will disappear or become a less pathogenic virus coexisting with a person.
