SARS-CoV-2 virus is characterized by a high cough speed or sneezing, as well as, perhaps, through direct contact with materials polluted by the virus.
The virus has also been found in feces, which means an oral-feccial method of transmission is also possible.
According to one of the most recent studies, 41% of cases of 138 scientists may have resulted from infection in the hospital, including 17 patients with other previously identified diseases and 40 medical personnel.
Thus, serious precautions should be taken to protect the population, especially health workers, social workers, their relatives, colleagues, and even outsiders who come into contact with patients or are infected.
The first line of defense in the struggle to reduce the risk of influencircturbation is the wearing of masks for the face; both surgical masks and respirators of the N-95 (Service No. 1860s) help to control the proprietance of viruses.
Surgical masks do not give microdollars of the fluid from potentially infected scatter in the air and settle on the surfaces from which they can be transmitted to others.
However, only N95 masks (Serpents No. 1860s) can protect against inhalation of viurions ranging in size from 10 to 80 nm, they transmit only 5% of virions; SARS-CoV-2 virus the same size as SARS-Co-CoV, and they are both about 85 nm.
Since the particles can penetrate even through the five surgical masks folded together, medical professionals in direct contact with patients must necessarily wear N95 masks (serial number 1860s), and not surgical masks.
In addition to masks, medical workers should wear a protective robe fitted on the figure in order to additionally reduce the possibility of contact with the viruses.
Viruses can also encroach into the body through the eyes.
On January 22, 2020, the doctor contracted SARS-CoV-2 despite the fact that he wore a N95 mask; perhaps the virus got into his body through his inflamed eyes.
Therefore, medical professionals should also wear transparent facial shields or closed-type protective glasses.
It is urgently urged to wash their hands with disinfecting detergents more often or potentially endangered populations in affected or potentially threatened populations, trying not to get out of their homes by self-isolation, and to limit contacts with potentially infected people.
Acceptable Distance to the Patient is a distance of about a meter.
These measures effectively reduce the risk of influencing and prevent the virus from spreading.
Despite the fact that the SARS-CoV-2 virus was previously not known to mankind, a high degree of its similarity with SARS-CoV, as announced on January 7, 2020, was supposed to be an alarming signal for China, taking into account the experience he gained during the SARS outbreak in 2003.
However, only after January 19 of 2020, the Director of the Center for disease control in Wuhan reassured citizens, stating that the new virus has low contagiousness and limited reproductive in human-to-human transmission, and that preventing the spread and control of the disease will not be a problem.
This statement greatly reduced social tensions, especially at a time when the whole country was preparing to celebrating the Chinese New Year, and a critically important time was missed, when the disease could be deteriorated within Wuhan with minimal losses.
China's health authorities can assimilate this harsh lesson and make significant improvements in the future.
For example, the health authorities should: (1) make more careful public statements, since every word is taken into account by citizens and can affect their attitudes and decisions; (2) to more carefully monitor and respond to the unusual information coming from clinics, not to await official reports from doctors and officials; (3) take more resolute measures to curb a potential epidemic in the early stages of its development, not to reassure the public; and (4) to carry out purposeful and effective exercises to raise awareness.
The COVID-19 flare caused by previously unexploded coronavirus severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2), began at the end of December 2019.
In less than another two months the disease engulfed the entire whole of China and at the time of drawing up this document was distributed in 50 countries around the world.
Since the virus is very similar to severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus (SARS-CoVV) and symptoms of COVID-19 are similar to the symptoms of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS), SARS recurrence occurred with an outcrang of COVID-19.
However, there are some significant differences between COVID-19 and SARS in terms of deterrence of the epidemic and treatment of patients.
COVID-19 affects older people more than young people and men more than women; the rate of severe and death rates among the elderly are also higher than among young people.
Mortality as a result of SARS is higher than COVID-19 (10.91% compared to 1,44%).
Patients with COVID-19 propagate the virus even in an asymptomatic course of the disease, while patients with SARS usually infect others only in severe cases of the disease, so it is more difficult to restrain the spread of COVID-19 rather than SARS.
This partially explains why SARS-CoV-2 spreads much faster and larger than SARS-CoV.
In some patients with COVID-19, the usual samples of SARS-CoV-2 can be negative.
On the other hand, the cured patients can again show positive tests for the virus.
All this significantly increases the risk of the spread of the virus.
Against the backdrop of such a rapid promotion of COVID-19 studies, some of the most important issues remain undecided, namely:
What’s the origin of SARS-CoV-2?
“Despite the discovery of a 96 per cent thomatologist between SARS-CoV-2 and two SARS-similar bacterial irregularities, we still can’t say that SARS-CoV-2 infection comes from bats.
Which animal became an intermediary species that transferred the virus from the original host, for example, from bacterial mice, to humans?
Unleaudned of answers to the first and second questions, we can not reliably interrupt the route of transmission, and the epidemiological situation can escalate at any time.
Solutions at the Molecular And Biochemological Samples demonstrated that SARS-CoV-2 binds with ACE2, but how exactly does the virus are introduced into the cells of the respiratory tract and provoke subsequent pathological changes?
Does the virus also bind to ACE2-producing cells in other organs?
Without a clear answer to these questions, we cannot provide a rapid and accurate diagnosis and an effective treatment.
How long the Epidemiary Will Last?
How does the virus genetically evolve during human-to-human transmission?
Will it cause a global pandemic, will it also disappear like SARS or periodically re-receiving like the influenza?
These aspects are of paramount importance, but it will take time to look for answers to these and many other questions.
However, we do not have any other choice other than, no matter what it costs, to stop the epidemic as soon as possible and to return to a normal life.
Zoonic origin of human coronavirus
Mutation and adaptation stimulated coronavirus (CoV) co-evolution (CoV) and their carriers, including people, for millennium.
Until 2003, it was known that two human coronavirus (HCoV) caused a mild disease, such as an ordinary common cold.
Flamethrowing Sharp Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) and Middle Eastern Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) have changed our understanding of how disastrous and life-hazard infection a human coronavirus can be.
The ad Appearance of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) coronavirus (SARS-CoV-2) at the end of 2019 again drew attention to the coronavirus and surprised us with high transmittance, but the weaker pathogenic pathogenicity of this virus compared to the related SARS-CoV virus.
Human coronavirus infection is zoonosis, and an understanding of its zoonotic origin will be very useful.
Most human coronaviruses come from bactericles, for which they are not pathogenic.
Intermediate carrier tanks of certain human coronaviruses are also known.
Definition of animal-carriers directly affects the prevention of the spread of human diseases.
Examination of the interaction of animals that carry coronaviruses can also shed light on the pathogenesis of the coronavirus in humans.
In this review, we certify available data on seven human coronaviruses, focusing on the history of their detection, as well as their zoonotic origin and methods of interspecific transmission.
It is important to note that we draw a comparison and comparison of various human coronaviruses in terms of viral evolution and genomic recombination.
In this context, the current epidemiological disease caused by the coronavirus, discovered in 2019 (COVID-19), is also being considered.
In addition, there are the conditions necessary for a successful replacement of carriers, and the effect of the evolution of the virus on the severity of the course of the disease.
The coronaviruses belong to the Coronaviridae family, which is a group of envelope viruses with a single positive-polar RNA strand.
These viruses with the largest RNA-containing viruses genome, which accommodating from 26 to 32 thousand nucleotides, were given due to their shape, which when viewed under an electron microscope resembles a coronation.
From the point of view of the structure, coronaviruses contain unsegregated genomas with an identical organization.
Approximately two-thirds of the geum contains two large indistinguishing reading frames (ORF1a and ORF1b), which are translated into pp1a replectins pp1a and pp1ab.
These polyproteins are further recyclable in order to generate 16 unstructured proteins denoted nsp1~16.
The remainder of the geum contains an open reading framework for structured proteins, including spiny-shaped squirrel (S), envelope protein (E), memabry (M) and nucleoprotein (N).
In other coronavirus lines also encoded a number of lines of specific accesorated protein.
Based on the differences in the protein sequence, coronaviruses are divided into four types (alpha, beta, gamma and deltacoronaviruses), the type of beta coronavirus includes most human coronaviruses and is divided into four lines (A, B, C and D).
There is phylogenotic evidence that bats and rodents of most alpha and beta coronaviruses are bats and rodents, while poultry is the main reservoir for gamma and trace tocoronavirus.
Thousands, coronaviruses continually overcame interspecific barriers, and some of them have evolved to pathologies dangerous for humans.
To date, seven human coronaviruses are known.
These include human alpha coronavirus HCoV-229E and HCoV-NL63.
The other five betacoronaviruses include HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1, severe acute respiratory coronavirus (SARS-CoV), Middle-Wedthnastic Respiratory Syndrophy (MERS-CoV) coronavirus and severe acute respiratory syndrome SARS-CoV-2 coronavirus.
Human coronaviruses HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU and HCoV-NL63 usually cause symptoms of a mild form, such as chasles and (or) diarrhea.
Contrary to them, SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV viruses and recently detected SARS-CoV-2 are extremely pathogenic, cause severe infections of the lower respiratory uterus in a comparatively larger number of patients more likely to develop acute respiratory disease (HRDs) and the manifestation of extrapulmonary symptoms.
The first filament of human coronavirus HCoV-229E, B814, was obtained from a sample separated from the nasopharyngial patients who had a cold in the 1960's.
Since then, extensive studies have accumulated a more detailed knowledge about HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43 viruses, which cause symptoms of a local infection.
In fact, before the outburst of severe spicy respirator syndrome (SARS), there was a generally accepted concept, according to which human coronavirus influencing was generally considered harmless.
SARS flare in 2003 was one of the most disgusting epidemiological epidemics in current history; more than 8,000 people were ill, and the total mortality rate was about 10 per cent.
Ten years later, the Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) flank led to a prolonged epidemic in the Arabian Peninsula, while the disease sporocadically spread to the rest of the world.
Discovered in 2019, a new human coronavirus (2019-nCoV), which was later renamed SARS-CoV-2, is the causative agent of the current coronavirus epidemic of coronavirus infection 2019 (COVID-19), which as of March 3, 2020 claimed more than 1,200 lives, and the number of infected exceeded 91,000 people.
Almighty signal has been obtained, and the world must prepare for the upcoming SARS-CoV-2 pandemic.
All seven human coronaviruses are of zoonotic origin, their sources are bats, mice or domestic animals.
Numerous datasets suggest in favor of the enovetic origin of all human coronaviruses from bacterial mice, to the bodies of which the viruses are well adapted and do not show pathogenic properties, but demonstrate a wide genetic diversity.
Epidemic of COVID-19 has left China and the world a slentifully medical, scientific, social and moral point of view.
The study of the zoonotic mechanism of the origin of human coronaviruses will allow us to understand their natural history, the driving forces of their evolution and the factors that limit interspecies transfer.
Perhaps it will also prompt or accelerate the search for a tank, intermediate and amplifying carrier SARS-CoV-2, which is very important to prevent the redissption of the disease in the future.
This review contains general information about zoonotic origin, interspecific transmission and pathogenesy of human coronaviruses.
In particular, we denote and consider the following general feature: the initial viruses from which human coronaviruses occurred are usually not pathogenic for their tank carriers, but become pathogenic after an interspecific transmission to a new nervosa.
We are also analysing the evolution of human coronavirus trends, according to which the increase in transmission is often accompanied by a weakening of pathogenic properties.
In the Same Context, the result of the current SARS-CoV-2 outbreak is also being discussed.
Animal coronaviruses are known since the end of the 1930-ies.
Prior to the B814 HCoV-229E virus, it was first obtained from a sample disulated from the nasopharyngeal patients with a cold, various coronaviruses were found in various infected animals, including turkeys, mice, crusts, pigs, cats and dogs.
In recent decades, seven human coronaviruses have been identified.
In table 1 informatively and visually presents a short history of the detection of human coronaviruses in chronological order.
The first HCoV-229E strain was obtained from samples taken from the respiratory true of patients with infectious lesions of the upper respiratory uterus in 1966; later this virus was adapted to multiplying in pulmonary pulmonary pulmonary pulmonary pulmonary pulmonary respiration.
In patients infected with HCoV-229E, cold symptoms appeared, including headache, sneezing, general malaise and sore in the sore throat, even though a high temperature and cough were observed in 10-20% of cases.
Subsequently, in 1967, HCoV-OC43 virus was isolated from the organ culture and the subsequent mass-produced passage to the brain of pin-membicum mice.
Clinical signs of HCoV-OC43 infection look similar manifestations of HCoV-229E infection, whose symptoms are indistinguishable from lesions of other respiratory way pathogens, such as influenza type A viruses and rhinoviruses.
Both HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43 virus are ubiquitously distributed in the world and, as a rule, are transmitted in winter in temperate latitudes.
Basically the incubation period of these two viruses lasts less than a week, followed by approximately a two-week-old disease.
According to the results of the study on volunteers, in healthy people infected with HCoV-229E, an ordinary cold of mild form has developed.
Only a few patients with imprenuled immensity showed a severe infection of the lower respiratory tract.
The SARS flare, also known as “atypical pneumonia epidemiology”, became the first well-documented pandemic in human coronavirus in human coronavirus, and the causative agent of the SARS-CoV virus turned out to be the SARS-CoV virus, the third identified human coronavirus.
The first SARS case was detected in late 2002 Guangdong Province of Guangdong.
During the SARS epidemic, 8,096 cases and 774 deaths were documented, and the disease has spread to many countries and on different continents.
Excluding super-active propagators, it was estimated that each patient can infect about another two people; the incubation period ranged from 4 to 7 days, and the peak of the viral load accounted for the tenth day of the disease.
In patients infected with SARS-CoV, there were initially pains in the muscles, headaches, high fever, general maligation and chills, and among the later symptoms there were short-fast, caccess and respiratory insufficiency.
Disseminated deviations of laboratory indicators from the norm of SARS are lymphopenia, disorders in the hepatic samples and an increased level of creatine kinase.
Patients with SARS have diffuse alveolar urinary urination, proliferation of epithelial cells and an increase in macrophages.
Approximately 20-30% of patients subsequently need intensive therapy and pulmonary voluntench.
In such severe cases, in addition to the lower respiratory tract, other organs can also be infected, including gastrointestinal tract organs, the liver and kidneys, which is usually accompanied by a cytokine storm, which may turn out to be lethal, especially for patients with imprenuled immunities.
For the first time the virus was singled out from an open pulmonary biopsy, a relative of the patient, who came to Hong Kong from Guangzhou.
Since then, huge efforts have been aimed at studying human coronaviruses.
At the end of 2004 HCoV-NL63 was separated by a 7-month-old child from the Netherlands.
At the outburst, it was found to mainly influencing children of the elderly, the elderly and patients with weakened immunity and respiratory diseases.
For the disease caused by HCoV-NL63, such manifestations such as runny nose, conjunctivitis, high fever and bronchiolitis are characteristic.
In another independent study, it is the isolation of the same virus from the nasal material, taken from an 8-month-old boy from the Netherlands suffering from pneumonia.
The virus has been discovered in the Netherlands, but in fact it is widespread.
HCoV-NL63 is estimated to cause approximately 4.7% of widespread respiratory diseases, and the peak of the diseases caused by them occurs at the beginning of summer, spring and winter.
HCoV-NL63 is associated with obstructive laryngitis, also called croup.
HCoV-HKKU1 was isolated in Hong Kong in the same year from a 71-year-old man with pneumonia and bronchiolitis.
In addition to related pneumonia and bronchiolitis, HCoV-HKU1 is associated with exacerbation of astmatic diseases.
Like HCoV-NL63, HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43, the HCoV-HKU1 virus that causes mild respiratory diseases was detected around the world.
All these four human coronavirus, which cause concomitant infections, adapted well to people, and their mutation, resulting in high-pathogenic diseases, is usually unlikely, although the incidents have been and their causes are unknown, as in a rare example with a more virulent HCoV-NL63 subsimilar, which, according to recent reports, has caused a serious infection.
Typically, by acquiring the ability to be effectively transmitted and preserved in the human body, these human coronaviruses become less virulent or pathogenic.
Coronavirus of the Middle Eastern Respiratory Syndrome (MERS-CoV) was first singled out in 2012 in Saudi Arabia from a 60-year-old patient with acute pneumonia and renal insufficiency.
Most laboratory-confirmed cases took place in the Middle East, but in different European countries and Tunis there have been imported cases and distribution through episcopal secondary infections in close contact.
Another secondary flank with 186 confirmed cases took place in South Korea in 2015.
Clinical manifestations of the Middle Eastern Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) resemblance to severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) - both infections are characterized by progressive acute pneumonia.
In contrast to SARS, many patients with MERS also developed acute renal insufficiency, which still distinguishes MERS from other diseases caused by coronavirus-transmitted to humans.
More than 30% of patients have gastrointestinal symptoms such as diarrhoea and vomit.
As of February 14, 2020, more than 2,500 laboratory-confirmed cases of the Middle Eastern respiratory syndrome were registered with a high mortality rate (34.4%), so MERS-CoV is considered one of the deadliest viruses known to a person.
In the period from the middle to the end of December 2019 in Wuhan of the Chinese province of Hubei, clusters of patients with pneumonia have been identified, which now, looking back into the cerevious, is associated with an infection caused by the coronavirus of severe acute respiratory syndrome type 2 type (SARS-CoV-2).
The World’s World Health Organization has announced that an ongoing low-reinth tract infection caused by SARS-CoV-2 is a global public health hazard situation, and the disease itself has been called “coronavirus infection 19” (COVID-19).
As of March 3, 2020, there are 90,053 confirmed cases in the world, and the mortality rate is approximately 3.4%.
It should not be noted that the mortality rate in the Chinese province of Hubei is 4.2%, and abroad - 1.2%.
SARS-CoV-2, like SARS coronaviruses and MERS, causes a severe respiratory infection characterized by an increase in temperature, cough and shortness of breath.
In some patients,  Diarrhoea is also observed.
Pneumonia is one of the heaviest symptoms and is able to quickly turn into acute respiratory insufficiency syndrome.
Despite the similarity of SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2, taking into account high homology of nucleotide sequences (82%), these viruses, nevertheless, form different branches of a phylogenetic tree.
SARS-CoV-2 is obviously less pathogenic, but has a greater ability to transfer compared to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
Asymptomatic SARS-CoV-2 infections have been reported, suggesting the ability of this virus to spread rapidly around the world.
As a result of the comparison and comparison of SARS-CoV-2 with other six human coronaviruses, important similarities and disparities are rediscluded.
In the first, the coronavirus-transmitted to a person has a similar incubation period and the duration of the course of the diseases caused by them.
In this regard, SARS-CoV-2 demonstrates the same trend as the other six human coronaviruses.
Secondly, COVID-19 symptoms are between SARS-CoV and the four human coronaviruses that cause dissimilitant infections (i.e. HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-NL63).
On the one hand, the manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection are similar to those most often observed when infected with human coronaviruses that cause concommunicated infections, including non-special manifestations, mild symptoms or even the absence of symptoms.
On the other hand, as with SARS-CoV infection, a small subgroup of severe COVID-19 can be distinguished, although the ratio here is slightly lower.
Thirdly, SARS-CoV-2 also shows interesting regularities characteristic of both human coronaviruses that cause incommunicable infections and SARS-CoV.
On the one hand, SARS-CoV-2 transmittance is at least as high as human coronaviruses that cause concommunicated infections.
On the other hand, it has yet to be checked to be seen whether SARS-CoV-2 transmittance decreases with each subsequent transition of the virus (i.e., the infection of each next person), such in cases of SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
Finally, as well as other human coronaviruses that cause dissimilar infections, SARS-CoV-2 can be found in fecal samples.
