Before dissipating the animal origin of HCoV viruses, it will be useful to us to discuss the definitions and characteristics of the evolutionary, natural, intermediate, reproducing and reservoir carriers of HCoV viruses.
The animal serves as an evolutionary HCoV carrier, if it carries a closely related ancestor with the same high homology at the nucleotide sequence level.
An ancestral virus is usually well-adaptaged and nepotogenic in this carrier.
In the same way, the carrier-reservoir constantly tolerates HCoV for a long period of time.
In both cases, the carriers are naturally infected and are natural carriers of HCoV or its parent virus.
Contrary to this, if HCoV has only been placed in an intermediary right or so far when it was brought to a person, it has not been well adapted to a new carrier and is often pathogenic.
This intermediary carrier can serve as a zoonotic source of a human infection and play the role of an amplifying carrier, allowing the virus to replicate slowly, and then transmitted it to people, increasing the scale of infection of people.
HCoV can tolerate an imperishable influence if it cannot withstand an intermediary transmission.
Conversely, HCoV viruses can also adapt to an intermediate and even establish prolonged endemplation.
In this case, an intermediate carrier becomes a natural reservoir carrier.
