In addition to the different types of animal carriers, the three main factors on the side of the viruses also contributed to the interspecific barriers.
First of all, their relatively high rate of mutations in RNA replication.
Compared to other viruses with a single-chain RNA, the estimated frequency of coronavirus mutations can be considered "moderate-tall" with an average frequency of replacements for approximately 10-4 substitutions per year at one site 2, depending on the stage of adaptation of the coronavirus to new hosts.
The coronaviruses have corrective exocibonuclease, the removal of which leads to an extremely high frequency of mutating and attenuation or even non-viability.
Interestingly, nucleotide analog remdesivir suppresses replication of coronaviruses due to the fusion of exoribonuclease and RNA-dependent RNA polymerase.
Remdesivir is one of the most promising drugs against SARS-CoV-2, which must be checked during clinical trials.
Nevertheless, the incidence of coronavirus mutations is almost a million times higher THAN their carriers.
In addition, the frequency of the mutation can often be even higher if the coronaviruses aren’t well adapted to the carrier.
Compared to SARS-CoV with high mutation rate, SARS-CoV-2 mutates is apparently lower, which indicates a higher level of adaptation to people.
Presumably, this virus has already been adapted to another carrier who is close to a person.
