To reduce the amount of unnecessary contacts between people and animals, you need to abandon the culture of eating wild animals in some parts of China.
After the harsh trials of SARS, MERS and COVID-19 have become the best prepared-of-readiness and plan to responsive situations.
In fact, many viruses have existed on the planet for a very long period of time.
They remain in their natural casings, until it becomes possible to seize new territories.
“Although many properties of bacterician bacteriums are favorable for the propagation of viruses, if you teach people to stay away from them, it is possible to minimize the likelihood of a person’s contact with bats and other species of wild animals.
To better understand the ecology of coronaviruses and their natural carriers, constant epidemiological supervision of mammals is necessary, which will be useful to prevent coronavirus transmission from animals to a person and future outburst of diseases.
In conclusion, the most effective way to prevent viral zoonosis is to stay away from the ecological niches of natural zoonotic virus reservoirs.
Zoonotic cycling SARS-CoV-2 still lacks certain fragments.
In the first, if bumblebees transmitted the SARS-CoV-2 AN ancestral virus to pangolines, it is interesting to see under what circumstances bats and pangolins share the same ecological niche.
Secondly, if bats play a more direct role in transmission to a person, it is necessary to determine how people have come into contact with bacterician mice.
Thirdly, if the role of a true intermediate carrier plays a third mammal, then it is necessary to find out how it interacts with different species, including humans, bacterial mice and pangolines.
AT AN ANNOUNCER, as many mammals, including pets may be susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, both epidemiological surveillance and an experimental incommunicature should be carried out.
Whether it is a bleak, pangolin or other mammal, it is expected that in the future SARS-CoV-2 or its progenitor viruses will be determined in their natural carriers.
Substantial studies in this area will shed light on SARS-CoV-2 in animals, which will have an important effect on COVID-19 prevention and control in humans.
It is Necessary to update the diagnostic criteria for “identisions of suspected disease” and “confirmed cases” of COVID-19
On February 6, 2020, our group published a brief reference manual on the diagnosis and treatment of a new coronavirus infection in 2019 (2019-nCoV), which provides information on our accumulated experience and gives verified recommendations to deal with this pandemic around the world.
Coronavirus Infection 2019 (COVID-19), however, is a new disease, so our understanding and knowledge increased gradually on the basis of the current results of research and clinary experience; thus the diagnostic strategy and the therapeutic practices have also been continuously updated.
In this letter, we responded to one commentary on our recommendations and present the latest diagnostic criteria for “violations of suspected disease” and “confirmed cases” in accordance with the document “Recommendations for Diagnosis and Therapy for COVID-19” (the seventh version), issued by the National Committee for Health of the People's Republic of China.
In December of 2019 there was an outburst of a new coronavirus of 2019 (2019-nCoV), which now received the official coronavirus infection of 2019 (COVID-19), and the viral disease itself was called “serious acute respiratory syndrome 2” (SARS-CoV-2).
On March 11, 2020, WHO qualified COVID-19 as a pandemic.
In order to combat SARS-CoV-2 infection, our group has developed a concise reference manual and has published it online on the Military Medical Research website on February 6.
This publication attracted a lot of attention.
At the same time note that COVID-19 is a new disease, so our understanding and knowledge increased gradually on the basis of the current results of the studies and clinical experience; thus the diagnostic strategy and the therapeutic practice have also been constantly updated.
For example, in the period from January 16 to March 3, 2020, there were seven editions of the document “Recommendations on Diagnosing and Therapy for COVID-19”, which was published by the National Committee on Health of the People’s PRC (http://www.nhc.gov.cn/), and the content of some of its provisions was significantly changed.
Recently, a work prepared by Zhou with co-authors provided comments about our recommendations. They contained simple diagnostic proposals based on an existing clinical experience.
This work has added new practical testimonies to our recommendations, as well as provided valuable background information on this pandemic covering the whole world.
We confirm the high importance of their work and express our gratitude.
However, according to the latest edition of “Recommendations on Diagnosing and Therapy for COVID-19” (Teating Seventh Version), both on current studies and their work needs to be upgraded.
In accordance with the seventh issue of this document (dated March 3, 2020), it is Necessary to merge any one element from the characteristics of an epidemiological history with two clinical manifestations of the disease:
Epidemiological history: (1) the history of movement or residence in Wuhan and its surroundings or in other localities, where there have been cases of COVID-19 for 14 days before the manifestation of symptoms; (2) the history of contacts with patients infected SARS-CoV-2 (with a nucleic meccined medication); (3) a history of contact with patients with high-temperature or respiratorial symptoms
Clinitial manifestations: (1) high temperature and/or respiratory symptoms; (2) the presence of external manifestations of COVID-19 infection; (3) the total number of leukocytes is a normal or reduced number of lymphocytes at an early stage of symptoms.
Diagnosing for a confirmed medical event should be based on the case of suspected disease with the presence of one of the following pathogenetic or serological evidentiary evidence, namely: (1) the PCR test in real-time is positive for SARS-CoV-2;
It can also be noted that in the second (January (January 18, 2020) and the third (January 22, 2020) issues of the specified document were added a real-typical PCR test for nucleic-acidic acids in the respiratory tract or blood samples.
Pathogenetic diagnosis of a blood test was added in the fourth (January 27, 2020) and the fifth (February 8, 2020) issues; and then in the seventh issue the need for serological evidence was added.
These changes are based on the Continuing performance of researchers looking for an optimal nucleic detachment kit during a rapid diagnosis, as well as to analyze samples taken from the respiratory tract, including blood sampling, which increases the availability of various samples and contributes to the inclusion of positive test results for specific antibodies in the criteria for confirming the disease.
At the same time, there is more and more evidence regarding the need to exercise caution when treating patients with atypical symptoms and patients without pronounced symptoms.
Thus, the route map, presented in the work of Zhou, etc., must be renovated, since those who do not have clinical symptoms are classified as a group of “low risk”.
The system of assessments also needs to be refined when conducting further clinical practice and research.
In conclusion, it should be noted that we look forward to obtaining more direct evidence and encourage readers to leave their comments.
With regard to diagnosing “identisals” and “confirmed cases”, we invite readers to monitor and strictly comply with the latest recommendations that are given in the countries where they live.
Our team will also revitalize its recommendations in a timely manner in order to provide effective assistance.
