In order to prevent the spread of the disease, handwashing is recommended.
CDC (Center for Disease Control and Preference) also recommends hudding your hands with soap and water for for at least 20 seconds, especially after going to the toilet or with severe hand contaminants, as well as before meals, after jerk, cough or sneezing.
This is necessary, since, being outside the human body, the virus is exterminated by household soap, which reveals its protective shell.
In addition, if soap and water are inaccessible, CDC recommends using disinfectants to treat alcohol-based hands with an alcohol content of at least 60%.
WHO advises people to avoid touching the eyes, nobility, or mouth with dirty hands.
The surfaces can be disinfected with a number of solutions (on the surface of stainless steel, the disinfectant begins to act a minute after application) with a content of 62-71% ethanol, 50% isopropanol, 0.1% sodium hypochlorinate, 0.5% hydrogen peroxide, and 0.2–7.5% canodon iodine.
Other Component Components, such as benzolconium chloride and chlorhexidine gluconate, are less effective.
The CDC recommends that, in the event of calculating COVID or confirmed to an institution such as an office or a day-to-hospital, all areas of such room, including offices, toilets, public spaces, electronic equipment such as tablets, touch screens, keyboards, remote control panels and ATMs used by sick people have been disinfected.
Medical organizations are recommended when coughing or sneezing to cover your mouth and nose with your elbow or a napkin and immediately eject used hygiene items.
