Social distancivation (also known as physical distilling) is a measure to combat the infection aimed at slowing the spread of the disease by minimizing close contacts between people.
Security measures include quarantine, restrictions on travel, clunapping schools, workplaces, stadiums and shopping machinations.
People can use social distancing measures, staying at home by limiting trips, avoiding crowded places, using contactless greetings and physically disparaging from others.
Many regional governments, especially those severely affected by the outbursts, now prescribe or recommend social distancing.
The largest number of people, which can be collected in one place according to the recommendation of U.S. and health care organizations, has been quickly reduced from 250 people (in regions where there was no COVID-19 prevalence data), to 50 people, and at the one awtering to 10.
On March 22, 2020, Germany forbade to assemble into groups that included more than two. Elderly people and persons suffering from diseases such as diabetes, heart diseases, respiratory diseases, hypertension, and a weakened immune system, face an increased risk of contracting the virus in a serious form. CDC is advised to stay at home for as long as possible if there is an outburst of the disease in the region. At the end of March 2020.
The use of “social distancing” was understood in such a way that people should expose themselves to complete social isolation, instead of staying in contact with other people in alternative ways. Some agencies have published guidelines on sexual health that should be used during a pandemic.
Among others, recommendations have been made to have sexual intercourse only with their regular partners with whom you are living, and in whom you are sure that he does not have a virus and its symptoms.
Persons diagnosed with COVID-19 and those who are suspected that they are infected are recommended self-isolation at home.
Health institutions have published detailed instructions on proper self-isolation. Governments of many countries have also introduced mandatory or recommended that the entire population of the affected areas be quarantined on its own.
Persons at high-risk persons were prescribed strict quarantine.
Individuals who may have been in contact with COVID-19 infected or have recently been visited by a country or region significantly affected by the epidemic have been encouraged to quarantine within a period of 14 days of last possible contact.
Strategies to combat the outbreak include restraining the spread of the disease, its suppression or mitigating.
Contiguration of the spread of the disease is carried out in the early stages and aims to track and isolate those infected, as well as other measures of infection control and vacillation in order to stop the spread of the disease among the rest of the population.
At the stage where the spread of the disease is no longer possible, efforts are aimed at mitigating the consequences: measures are being taken to decelerate the diffusion and mitigating the impact of the epidemic on the health and social system.
Deterrence measures can be taken at the same time.
Residing the infection requires more extreme measures to reverse the pandemic, reducing the basic number of cases of influencing to a level below 1. Part of efforts to manage an infectious disease outbreak is aimed at reducing the peak of the epidemic, known as equalizing an epidemiological crucifix.
Such efforts reduce the risk of congestion of health services and provide more time to develop vaccinates and therapies.
Non-pharmaceutical interventions, which can help to cope with the outbreak, include measures of personal prevention, such as hand gigning, the use of medical masks and self-isolation; social measures aimed at physical distancing, such as closure of schools and the abolition of mass events; inquiring the community to assist and participating in such measures; as well as measures to protect the environment, such as cleaning the Surface.
Other countries have also adopted a number of measures aimed at limiting the spread of the virus.
In South Korea introduced mass screening and localized quarantines, as well as organized the operation of a warning system for the movements of infected persons.
In Singapore provided financial support to infected persons in self-isolation and imposed large fines on those who did not.
In Tayynana, the production of medical mascos and was fined for accumulators for the accumulation of excessive reserves of medicines. Simulation of the United Kingdom and the U.S. has shown that there are serious problems in terms of mitigating (slump, but not stopping the spread of the epidemic) and the suppression (stutting the growth of the epidemic) there are serious problems.
Optimal mitigating the consequences of the spread of the disease can reduce the peak burden on the health care system by 2/3, and mortality by halving, but still lead to a hundred thousand deaths and a collapse of health systems.
Residency may be the preferred method, but it needs to be used until the virus circulates among the population (or until a vaccine has been developed if this happens earlier), since otherwise the spread of the disease will quickly resume when the measures will be weakened.
Long-Tercivil Interference to Destrolate the Pandemic leads to social and economic costs.
Antiviral drugs approved to treat COVID-19 currently do not yet exist, but efforts are being made to develop them, including testing existing drugs.
Receiving unconsuming drugs for a cold, drinking a sufficient amount of fluid and rest can help to relieve symptoms.
Depending on the severity of the disease, the patient may require oxygen therapy, intravenous administration of fluids and support for respiration.
The use of steroid drugs can only harm.
Several compounds that have been previously approved for the treatment of other viral diseases are also being considered for use for the treatment of COVID-19.
WHO also reported that some “traditional and home remedies” can relieve symptoms caused by SARS-CoV-19.
World-rencies is viewing capacity-building and health-help adaptation to the needs of patients with COVID-19 as a major response to a disease outbreak.
The European Center for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC) and the WHO Regional Regional Office of Europe have issued guidelines for clinics and primary health services to assist in redistributing resources at several levels, including concentrating laboratory services on COVID-19 testing, analysing non-necessary procedures where possible to detect the virus and isolating patients diagnosed with COVID-19, as well as increasing retaliatory treatment facilities by training.
There are various theories about where the very first case of infection may have arisen - the so-called "zero patient".
The first well-known case of a new coronavirus infection, probably took place on December 1, 2019 in Wuhan, Hubei province, China.
During the month, the number of cases of coronavirus infection in Hubei province gradually increased.
Basically, they were associated with the Huangan wholesale of seafood, which also sold live animals, and one of the theories is that the virus entered the human body from one of these animals; in other words, the virus is of zoonotic origin. On December 26, a case of a mass pneumonia disease of unknown origin was registered in the clinic of Hubei, with whom the doctor Zhang Tyanjin worked, who reported this case.
On December 30, a group of doctors of the central clinic of Wuhan warned its colleagues about the “coronavirus, similar to SARS”.
Eight of these doctors, including Lee Wenlingan, were alerted by the police about responsibility for spreading false rumours, and the physician, Ai Feen, was reprimanded from her superiors for raising a panic.
Subsequently, on December 31, Ukhan’s Municipal Health Commission published a public notice and informed WHO about the current situation.
Wuhan’s health authorities reported the incidence of unknown pneumonia, which turned out great enough to initiate an investigation in the beginning of January.
In the beginning and mid-January of 2020 the virus also spread to other Chinese provinces, facilitated by the Chinese New Year holidays and the fact that Wuhan is a transport and main railway node.
January 20, China reported 140 new infections in a single-to-day infection, including two people who fell ill in Beijing and one in Shenzhen.
According to more recent official data, by January 20, 2020, 6,174 people of the disease appeared. As of March 26, the United States has overtaken China and Ithalia due to the largest number of confirmed cases in the world. As of April 9, 2020, more than 1.61 million cases of infection were registered worldwide, more than 97 000 people were killed, and more than 3,60,000 have recovered.
Approximately 200 countries and territories have recorded at least one reported case.
Because of the pandemic, many European Schengen countries have restricted free movement and established border controls.
National responses included measures to curb the spread of the disease, such as quarantine (known as being able to stay at a home, mandatory shelter at home or isolation), as well as curfew. As of April 2, about 300 million people, or about 90% of the population of the U.S., were quarantined; more than 50 million people are in isolation in the Philippines, about 59 million people are isolated in South Africa and 1.3 billion people in India.
On March 26, 1.7 billion people around the world were at some form of isolation, and two days later this figure increased to 2.6 billion people - about a third of the world's population - about a third of the world's population.
