Laboratory testing of respiratory coronavirus 2019 (COVID-19) and associated SARS-CoV-2 virus includes methods that reveal the presence of the virus, and methods for detecting antibosses in response to the infection.
The presence of viruses in the specimens is confirmed by OT-PCR, which recognizes coronavirus RNA.
This test is specific and is intended only to detect SARS-CoV-2 RNA.
It is used to confirm sufficiently recent or active infections.
Detection of antibodies (serology) can be used both for diagnosis and for the purpose of population control.
Analyses for antibodies reveal the number of people who have undergone the disease, including those whose symptoms were too insignificant to go to the hospital or were completely away.
Accurate level of mortality from this disease and the level of collective immunity can be determined by the results of such a test.
As of in March 2020, due to the limited possibilities of testing, there was no reliable data on the prevalence of the virus among the population.
By March 23, no country was able to verify more than 3% of their population, and information on the number of tests conducted in different countries is very contradictory.
It is likely that such disparities in information significantly affect recorded mortality rates, which in some countries can be significantly exaggerated.
With real-time reverse trancryping (rRT-PCR), the test can be carried out on samples from the respiratory tract obtained in various ways, including smear from the nasopharynx or smear sample smear.
The results are usually available for a period of several hours to 2 days.
OT-PCR test, conducted on smears, taken from the throat, is valid only during the first week of the disease.
Later the virus can disappear from the throat, continuing, nevertheless, to multiply in the lungs.
Infected patients tested in the second week of the disease, as an alternative, material from the lower respiratory tract can be taken using a sucking catheter, or it is possible to use expectoration products (sputum).
One of PCR's earlier tests was developed at the Charite clinic, Berlin, in January 2020 using a real-time reverse transcription polymer chain reaction (rRT-PCR) and formed the basis of 250 000 kits, which were subsequently disseminated by the World Health Organization (WHO).
By January 23, 2020, the United Kingdom also developed its test. On January 28, 2020, the South Korean company Kogenebiotech has developed a set to detect SARS-CoV-2 on the basis of Clinical PCR (PowerChek Coronavirus).
