At the present time there are about 18 000 such machines in the United States, and Abbott plans to increase the production of up to 5 000 tests per day. Currently, Taiwan develops a test using a monoclonal antibody, which in a specific way binds to a nucleocapsid protein of a new coronavirus, and there is some hope of obtaining results after 15 to 20 minutes, similar to an express test for influenza.
In a March 2020 literature review, it concluded that “chestal rengenograms have little diagnostic value in the early stages, whereas CT results [comcomputing tomography] can have such value even before the onset of symptoms.”
Typical specimens detected during CT include two-sided multi-played separial separating fosci on the type of matte glass with a peripheral, asymmetric and a posterior distribution.
Subpleural dominion, the symptom of a cobblestone pavement and consolidation develop as the disease progresses.
The results of a study comparing PCR and CT methods used in Wuhan at the time of the current pandemic have shown that CT CT is much more sensitivable than PCR, although it is less specific, since many of its imaging functions coincide with other processes for pneumonia and other diseases.
Since March 2020, the American College of Radiology has published a recommendation "not to use CT for screening or as a first-learth testing method in COVID-19 diagnosis". As of in March of 2020, CDC would recommend using the PCR method.
Partially the immune response to the infection is expressed in the production of antibodies, including IgM and Ig.
These antibodies can be used to detect infection in people after day 7 of the presence of symptoms, to determine the immune system and for the purpose of conducting population control. Analyses can be carried out in central laboratories (CLTs) or by nascifying on-site (PoCT)
In many clinical laboratories, these analyzes will be able to perform high-performance automated systems, but their accessibility will depend on the production rate of each such system.
The CLT typically uses one peripheral blood specimen, although serial specimulators can also be used to track an immune response.
With the PoCT, one blood sample is usually obtained by puncture of the skin.
In contrast to PCR methods, blood sampling stage is not required. On March 26, 2020 the FDA called 29 organizations that have passed all the necessary registration procedures and can now distribute their antibodies.
