Washing of the hands, also known as “hand hypogiene”, is the process of cleaning hands in order to eliminate contaminants, fats, microorganisms, or other harmful substances.
Regular hand washing with soap in certain "critical moments" during the day prevents the spread of many diseases, such as diarrhea and cholera, which are transmitted by the fecal and oral.
A person can also become infected with respiratory diseases, such as the flu or a common cold, for example, if it touches the eyes, nose or mouth (i.e. mucous membranes) unwashed hands.
Five critical moments during the day, after which you should wash your hands with soap: before and after defecation, after washes your buttension of the child or a change of diapers, before breastfeeding, before meals, as well as before and after the preparation of the measia or the processing of raw meat, fish and poultry.
