In the late 1990-ies and at the beginning of the 21 th century began to gain the popularity products for hand hygiene on an alcohol basis, not containing water (also known as hand disinfectants on an alcohol basis, antiseptic agents for hand disinfection or sanitizers).
Most of these products are made based on isopropyl alcohol or ethanol with the addition of thickants, such as carbomer (acrylic acrylic acid polymere) in the form of a gel, or humidifier, such as glycerin in a liquid or foam form, ensuring the convenience of using these agents and reducing the effect of driving of the skin with alcohol.
The addition of diluted hydrogen peroxide further increases antimicrobial activity. Disinfectants containing a minimum of 60-95% alcohol effectively destroy germs.
Disinfectants on an alcohol basis kill bacteriums, including multidrug-resistance bacteria (RMSA and VRE), tuberculosis bacillus, as well as some viruses (including HIV, herpes, RSV, rhinovirus, vaccinum, influenza and hepatitis) and fungi.
Disinfectants containing 70% alcohol kill 99.97% of bactericles (logarithm abbreviation of 3.5 similar to a reduction of 35 decibels on the hands 30 seconds after application and 99.99% - 99.999% of the bacteria (logarith a reduction of 4- to 5) on the hands 1 minute after application. Disinfectants are most effective against bacteria and are less effective against certain viruses.
