With a high-profile speech, there are more droplets into the air than with the speech of normal volume.
A study conducted in Singapore has shown that with an open cripple, the droplets can spread at a distance of up to 4.5 meters (15 ft).
Virus, as a rule, is not transmitted by air, but the National Academ of Sciences suggested that the transmission of virus particles by a bioaerous way is still possible, and testing air recycled by air collectors located in the corridors outside the rooms of people showed the presence of viral RNA.
Some medical procedures, such as intubation and cardiac resuscitation, can lead to dispensation of excretion products and, therefore, to the spread of the virus in the air.
There are also fears that the virus can spread through feces, however, this risk is considered to be low. The virus is the most contagious, when people have symptoms; the spread of the virus is possible before symptoms appear, but this risk is small.
Employees Employees of the European Center for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC) argue that it is not yet entirely clear how easily the virus spreads, however, it is known that one patient usually infects 2-3 other people. The virus is able to smear on the surface for several hours to a few days.
In particular, it was found that on the cardboard surface the virus is able to live for one day, on a plastic surface (polypropylene) and stainless steel (AISI 304) - for up to three days, and for 99% copper surfaces - up to 4 hours.
These indicators, however, vary according to motivity and temperature.
Soap and determinants when used correctly have a rather beneficial effect in terms of combating infection: soap destroys the fatty protective layer of the virus, deactivating it in this way, and is also able to eliminate it from the surfaces of the skin and other surfaces.
Other solutions, such as benzalonic chloride and chlorhexidine gluconate (surgical disinfectant), are less effective against this virus. During a study conducted in Hong Kong salivary, salivary specimens were taken an average of two days after hospitalization.
In five of the six patients, the first test showed the tallest concentration of the virus in the blood, and in the sixth of a patient, the tallest content of the virus in the blood was detected on the second day of testing.
Severe Acute Respiratory coronavirus syndrome 2 (SARS-CoV-2) - a new severe acute coronavirus respiratory syndrome first detected in three people with pneumonia from a group with acute respiratory diseases registered in Wuhan.
All specs of the new SARS-CoV-2 virus are also found in related coronaviruses.
Being outside the human body, the virus is exterminated by household soap, which reveals its protective envelope. SARS-CoV-2 is closely connected to the original SARS-CoV.
Lung is the organ most susceptible to COVID-19, as the virus enters host cells through angiotensin-conspiring enzyme 2 (ACE2) that is most precipitated in lung type II alveolary cells.
The virus connects with ACE2 and penetrates the host cage using a characteristic superficial glycoprotein - "wheelpiece".
In 12% of infected people admitted to hospital in Wuhan was diagnosed with acute myocardial damage, which is more common in a severe form of the disease.
The frequency of cardinal symptoms is high due to a systemic inflammatory response and impaired immune system observed during the progression of the disease, but acute myocardial lesions may also be associated with the presence of ACE2 receptors in the heart.
Receptors ACE2 are present in the heart in large quantities, since they are involved in the work of this organ.
A high thrombosis frequency (31%) and venous thromboembolism (25%) was observed in patients with COVID-19 and may indicate unfavourable prognoses. An autopsy of COVID-19 patients showed diffuse alveolar malveolar damage (DAD) and lymphocyte-containing inflammatory inflammatory inflammatory maltrets in pulmonary pulmonary.
Despite the fact that SARS-COV-2 has tropicism for ACE2-expressing epithelial cells of the respiratory tract, in patients with a severe course of COVID-19 have symptoms of systemic hyperinflammation.
In particular, it was revealed that pathogenic GM-CSF T-cells are correlated with the recruitment of inflammatory monocytes releasing an IL-6 and a severe pulmonary pulmonary pathology in patients with COVID-19.
Lymphocytic infiltators were also detected during the autopsy.
The WHO has also published several testing protocols for this disease.
Real-Time Polymerase Chaint Respondence (PP-PCR) is a standard method of testing.
The test is usually carried out on respiratory specimens obtained from the nasopharyngeal smear, but a smear or sputum sample can also be used.
The results are usually prepared for a period ranging from several hours to two days.
You can also take blood tests, but they require two blood samples, taken at an interval of two weeks, and their results do not have a direct value.
Chinese scientists managed to single out a strain of the coronavirus and publish its genetic sequence, so that scientists in laboratories around the world can independently develop polymerase chain reaction (PCR) tests to detect the presence of this virus.
As of April 4, 2020, antibody tests, which could detect the presence of an infection at the moment, as well as the fact of possible infection in the past, were in the stage of development, but have not yet become widespread.
Chinese experience of studying the results of these tests showed that their accuracy is only 60-70%.
On March 21, 2020, the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved the first on-study diagnostic test, authorizing its use at the end of this month. The guidelines for diagnosis published by Zhongnan Hospital of Wuhan University are indicated methods for detecting an infection based on clinical and epidemiological attance.
