Subpleural dominance, the symptom of a cobblestone pavement (the long-term thickening of the partition with alternating alveolar filling), and the consolidation develops as the disease progresses.
Microscopic lesions and pathophysiology of COVID-19 have little data.
The main results of a pathological study, carried out during the ascension:
Macroscopy: pleurisy, pericarditis, compaction and pulmonary edema
You can observe four types of severity of viral pneumonia:
slight form of pneumonia: pulmonary swelling, pneumatic hyperplasia, large atypical pneumocytes, interstitial inflammation with lymphocytic infiltration and the formation of multi-nucleated gigantic cells
Severe pneumonia: diffuse alveolar damage (DAD) with diffuse alveolar exudate
DAD is caused by acute respiratory dystress syndrome and severe hypoxemy.
healing pneumonia: organizing exudates in alveolar cavities and pulmonary interstitial fibrosis
Blood: diffuse intra-vascular coagulation (DHS-syndronomy); leukaerroblastic reaction
Prophylactic measures to reduce the probability of infection include staying at home, avoiding crowded places, frequent handwashing with soap and water for at least 20 seconds, adherence to the rules of respiratory hygiene, as well as avoiding touching the eyes, nose or mouth with unbnured hands.
The CDC recommends covering your mouth and nose with a nap when coughing or sneezing, or, in the absence of a napkin, cover the mouth and nose with the inner side of the elbow area of the hand.
After a cough or sneezing, it is advisable to carry out a quality hygienic procedure for the hands.
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends the use of tissue facial masks, in particular in order to limit the transmission of infection by persons with asymptomatic course of the disease. social distancing strategies are aimed at reducing the contact of infected patients with large groups of people; as part of these measures, schools and enterprises have been closed, the movement of citizens has been limited and large public measures have been canceled.
Distancing guidelines also implicate that people must be at least 6 feet (1.8 m) from each other.
Medications with confirmed efficacy against COVID-19 does not exist. Since the completion of the development of the vaccine is expected no earlier than 2021, the majority of measures against the spread of COVID-19 is reduced to a decrease in the peak of the epidemic, known as an “exit to the plateau”.
The CDC also recommends washing your hands with soap and water for at least 20 seconds, especially after visiting the toilet or with severe contamination of the hands, as well as before meals and after jerk, cough or sneezing.
CDC also recommends the use of disinfecting alcohol-containing (an alcohol content - at least 60%) a hand treatment agent, but only in cases where soap and water are inaccessible. Regions where such disinfectants are not forgivable, WHO provides two recipes for local production.
In these compounds use ethanol or isopropane, which have antimicrobial activity.
hydrogen peroxide is used to eliminate bacterial sprouts in alcohol and is not a suitable tool for antiseptic hand treatment.
Glycerol is added as a humidifier.
The patients are shown supportive therapy, which can include infusion therapy, oxygen support, as well as the support of other impoverished vital organs.
CDC advises those who are suspected to be infected with the virus to wear an ordinary medical mask.
Extracorporal memabrannic oxigenation (ECMO) was used to address respiratory insufficiency, but its benefits are still being studied.
To strengthen the immunity, it is recommended to observe the rules of personal hygiene, a healthy way of life and a diet.
Support-edning the therapies can be indicated for patients with mild symptoms in the early stages of the disease. WHO and the National Health Commission of China have published recommendations for caring for patients hospitalized with COVID-19.
In the United States, intensive care and pulmonologists have summarized the therapeutic recommendations of various institutions in a free resource - IBCC.
As in April 2020, there is no specific treatment for COVID-19.
For the treatment of symptoms as first-line drugs, some medical professionals recommend choosing in favor of paracetamol (acetaminofen) compared with ibuprofen.
When executing procedures during which airborne ejection, such as intubation or hand drying, it is necessary to take precautions to minimise the risk of transmitting the virus, especially in medical institutions.
CDC recommends placing an airborne compliant (AIIR) – an additional measure in addition to standard contact and air safety precautions. CDC published recommendations on the use of personal protective equipment (PPE) during a pandemic.
Appropriate means of individual protection: a protective bathrobe, respirator or a medical mask, means of eye protection and medical gloves are preferable to use respirators, not medical masks.
Respirators N95 are approved for use in an industrial environment, but the FDA authorized the use of these respirators according to an emergency authorization (EUA).
They are designed to protect against particles in the air, such as dust, but when used not according to an instruction, efficiency in terms of protection against a specific biological agent is not guaranteed.
If medical disguises are unavailable, the CDC recommends using protective face screens or, as a last resort, to make masks on your own at home.
In most cases, COVID-19 forms are not difficult enough to require artificial ventilation of the pulmonary volumination or its alternative, but there is also a certain percentage of cases where this is necessary.
The type of respiratory support for hospitalized patients with COVID-19 respiratory insufficiency is currently being actively explored, while there is some evidence that intubation could be avoided by nasal oxygen chanulium with an intensive air flow or a two-tier positive respiratory pressure.
It’s notorieting yet whether any of these two methods for patients in a critical condition is as effective as ventilator.
Some doctors make a choice in favor of an invasive machinable pulmonary ventilator, if it is available, since this method significantly limits the propagation of particles in the air compared with nasal cannula with an intense air flow. The risk of a severe form of the disease for the elderly (those who are older than the age of 60, and especially those who are older than the age of 80) is much higher.
In many developed countries there is a shortage of hospital beds per capita, and the resources of health systems are too limited to cope with a sharp increase in the incidence of COVID-19 in a severe form requiring hospitalization.
According to the results of one study conducted conducted in China, 5% of the patients were hospitalized in intensive care units, 2.3% were in need of pulmonary support, and 1.4% died.
Approximately 30% of people hospitalized with COVID-19 ended up in intensive care.
The task of ensuring artificial pulmonary ventilation becomes more complicated, since acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS), which develops with COVID-19, and oxygenation entails more and more problems.
ventilators with inhalation-intensive ventilates and MPCV are necessary to deliver the maximum amount of oxygen to the lungs and to provide a minimum volitional veterinary damage, which can develop pneumothorax.
