Its application was not subjected to clinical studies, which should be carried out to prove the safety and efficacy of the method of treatment.
In February 2020, China launched a mobile application designed to combat an outbreak of the disease.
To enter, the users must enter their own name and identification number.
The application can detect “close contact” using observational data and therefore identify the potential risk of infection.
Each user can also verify the status of three other users.
If a potential hazard is found, the application not only recommends self-isolation, but also sends a notification to local health authorities. Analysis of big data collected from mobile phones, facial recognition technologies, mobile phone tracking, and artificial intelligence - such tools are being used to track infected people and persons with whom they contacted in South Korea, Taiwan and Singapore.
In March 2020, the Israeli government gave permission to security agencies to track the data of mobile phones of people allegedly infected with the coronavirus.
Efforts have been taken to provide quarantine and protection for persons who may be in contact with infected citizens.
