For the quantification of the mortality rate, several methods are usually used.
All figures vary according to the region and the timing of the spread of the disease, they are also affected by the volume of testing, the quality of health systems used treatment regimens, the time since the onset of the disease outbreak and the parameters of the population, such as age, sex and general health.
At the end of 2019, WHO assappropriated ICD-10: U07.1 for death from laboratory-confirmed SARS-CoV-2 and U07.2 for deaths from clinical or epidemiologically diased COVID-19 without laboratory confirmation of SARS-CoV-2.
According to Johns Hopkins University statistics, as of April 17, 2020, the global number of deaths and cases of infection is 6.9% (153 822/2 240 191).
This figure varies depending on the region. Some other methods include the determination of a disease mortality coefficient (CFR), which reflects the percentage of diagnosed patients who have died as a result of the disease, as well as the determination of a mortality rate resulting from an infection (IFR), which reflects the percentage of infected patients (both diagnosed and undiagnosed cases) who have died as a result of the disease.
These statistical figures are not tied to a specific time and reflect indicators of a certain stratomevictory from the moment of influencision to the end of the course of the disease.
