Pandemic influence and mortality rates are different for men and women.
According to studies carried out in China and Italy, the mortality rate is higher in men.
The most at-risk group are men over the age of more than the age of 50; the gap between men and women has only narrowed since the age of 90.
In China, the mortality rate was 2.8 per cent for men and 1.7 per cent for women.
Accurate causes of this divergence are unknown, but may lie in the genetic and behavioral factors.
Gender immunological dissimilar, the lower prevalence of smoking among women, concomitant diseases in men (such as hypertension manifested in men at a younger age than in women) can be the cause of a higher mortality among men.
In Europe, 57% of those infected were men, and 72% of COVID-19 deaths accounted for men.
As of April 2020, the U.S. government does not maintain gender statistics on COVID-19.
Scientific studies have shown that viral diseases such as Ebola, HIV, influenza and SARS have different gender statistic.
