On February 11, 2020, the World Health Organization announced the official name for COVID-19.
The head of the WHO, Mr. Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus, explains: “CO” means “crown”, “VI” – “virus”, “D” – “disease), and the year 19 was first detected: December 31, 2019.
Such a name was chosen to avoid references to a specific geographical location (for example, China), animal species, or groups of people, as required by international naming recommendations aimed at preventing stigmatization. The virus that causes COVID-19 is called severe acute coronavirus respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
In public messages, WHO additionally uses “COVID-19 virus” and “virus responsible for COVID-19”.
Both the disease and the virus itself are usually referred to as “coronavirus”.
During an initial outburst in Wuhan, China, the virus and disease were commonly referred to as a “coronavirus” and “Huhank coronavirus”.
In January of 2020, in accordance with the recommendations of 2015 on the use of geographical locations in diseases and viruses, the WHO recommended the use of “acute respiratory disease 2019-nCov” and “2019-nCoV” as temporary names of the virus and the disease.
On February 11, 2020, the official names “COVID-19” and “SARS-CoV-2” were published.
Due to the limitations of standard supply strands, some manufacturers of digital services print medical materials, such as tampons to take smears out of the nose, as well as parts of ventilators.
In one of such cases, the Italian clinic urgently needed a ventilation valve, and the supplier was unable to deliver it in the required time, then one of the local industries was repurposed and was able to print the necessary 100 valves overnight.
After a COVID-19 flare erupted for for the first time, various conspiracy theories, disinformation and fake information regarding the origin of the virus, its scale, prevention, treatment and other aspects began to spread at a high speed.
People are likely to indulge other animals with a virus.
The results of the studies did not confirm the spread of the virus among swine pigs, ducks and chickens.
At the moment, there is no approved vaccine against the virus or drugs for its treatment.
Currently, governmental organizations, academial groups and industry researchers are conducting a variety of international studies of COVID-19 vaccine and drugs.
In MY MY March, the World Health Agency initiated the SOLIDARITY Trial program, which aims to assess the therapeutic effect of the four existing compounds considered the most effective to date.
The finished vaccine does not yet exist, but various organizations are actively developing candidate drugs for a vaccine.
Both SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 permeates into human cells using a ACE2 receptor, therefore, scientific developments use the results of previous SARS-CoV studies.
There are three vaccination strategies.
Firstly, the researchers strive to create a whole ivirion vaccine.
The use of such a virus, whether an inactive or a dead form, is aimed at inducing a rapid immune response of the human body to a new COVID-19 infection.
The second strategy, creating a subunited vaccine, is aimed at creating a vaccine, which increases the sensitivity of the immune system to certain subunits of the virus.
In the case of SARS-CoV-2, such studies are aimed at the demolition of the S spike protein, which helps the virus to penetrate the ACE2 enzyme receptor.
The third strategy is the nucleic acacine vaccine development (DNA or RNA Vaccine), a new method of creating a vaccine.
Expiliate vaccines developed on either of these strategies must be tested for safety and efficacy. On March 16, 2020 in Seattle began the first clinical trial of the vaccine on four volunteers.
Vaccine contains a harmless genetic code excavated from the virus, which causes the disease. The anti-telosive amplification was indicated as one of the potential problems in the development of SARS-COV-2 vaccines, but this assumption has a controversial nature.
As in April 2020, more than 300 active clinical trials are being carried out.
Medications, approved for the treatment of malaria were evaluated during seven tests, four of these studies were hydroxychloroqui or chloroquine.
Reprofiling of antiviral drugs accounts for a large part of Chinese studies; by the end of April, nine Phase III studies on remdesivire have been carried out.
As of April 2020, a dynamic review was carried out on the clinical development of Vaccine and Candidate Drugs for COVID-19. To the Same Purpose, several existing antiviral drugs were also being considered to treat COVID-19, including remdesivir, chlorochine and hydroxychloroquine, lopinovir / ritonavir, and lopinavir / ritonavir combined with interferon beta.
As of March 2020 there are preliminary data on the efficacy of remdesivir.
Clinical improvement was observed in patients receiving remdesivir as an exception.
Stage III clinical trials has already been taking place in the U.S., China, and Italy. Chlorochine, which was previously used to treat malaria, has been discussed in China in February 2020, and there are preliminary results.
Nevertheless, there is a need for an expert assessment of the study.
The health authorities of Korea and China recommend using chlorophyne.
The Wuhan Institute of Virology recommends a daily dose of one graduation, but at the same time notes that the doubling of this dose is very dangerous and can lead to a fatal outcome.
On March 28, 2020 the FDA issued an emergency use of hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine at the discretion of doctors treating patients with COVID-19. The Chinese instructions of the 7th edition also indicate interferon, ribavirin or mumphenovir, as a remedy against COVID-19.
Preliminary data indicate that high doses of ribavirin are necessary to inhibit SARS-CoV-2 in vitro.
Nitazoksanide was recommended for a detailed study in vivo, demonstrating SARS-CoV-2 hiccups in low concentrations. Studies have shown that for the penetration of SARS-CoV-2 by interacting with the ACE2 receptor, it is necessary to reconcile transmembrean core protease protease 2 (TMPRSS2).
Studies of using chlorochine and hydroxychloroquine and hydroxychlorochin with or without azithromycin have serious limitations, which do not allow the medical community to use these methods of treatment without a more detailed study. Ozeltamivir does not inhibit SARS-CoV-2 in vitro, and its role in the treatment of COVID-19 is obscure.
